# Fire a Wand of Animal Summoning and let the screen name what arrives.
# Finding PA-08-F13 of bd inc-tek.8.8. Read by tools/check_wand_animal.sh.
#
# THE DEFECT. The wand's data entry passes xval: MA_DRAGON, copied verbatim
# from the Dragon Summoning wand directly above it in lib/m_items.irh.
# Magic::Summon copies xval into xe.enConstraint (src/Effects.cpp:1451) and
# that is the monster-type filter, so the wand summoned from the wrong list.
#
# THE ORACLE is the line Magic::Summon prints for every creature that lands,
# "An <Obj> appears!" (src/Effects.cpp:1493). The check reads the creature's
# name back off the screen and looks its MTypes up in lib/mon*.irh.
#
# The steps, in order:
#
#   w y                      enter wizard mode; 'y' answers the one-time
#                            "enter wizard mode?" question.
#   Wizard Mode Switches     the wizard switches are OPT_NODEATH (801) through
#   DOWN*7 SPACE             OPT_FREEZE_MON (811) in order (inc/Defines.h:
#   DOWN*3 SPACE ESC         4137-4147), so row 8 is Infinite Mana and row 11
#                            is Freeze Monsters. SPACE toggles and does not
#                            move the cursor.
#                            Infinite Mana, because a barbarian's 17 mana pays
#                            for two shots and the run needs eight.
#                            Freeze Monsters, so nothing walks up and attacks
#                            while the wand is fetched, and nothing writes
#                            over the summon message.
#   Item Acquisition         RIGHT*4 walks the glyph row to Wands
#   RIGHT*4                  (ItemGlyphs, src/Tables.cpp:320).
#   @until "Wand of          the candidate list has no menu letters, so the
#     Goblinoid Summoning"   cursor is walked. Three rows show and the MIDDLE
#     DOWN                   one is selected, so the run stops on the pass
#                            where the row BELOW the wanted one appears.
#                            Goblinoid Summoning is the entry after Animal
#                            Summoning in lib/m_items.irh. That is an ordering
#                            assumption and the check does not trust it: it
#                            reads the wand's name back off the screen first.
#   ENTER 5 ENTER            take it at plus 5. "Enter Item Level:" sets the
#                            inherent plus (src/Debug.cpp:613-622). pval is
#                            PLUS_2PER1, so the summon gets a CR budget of 10.
#                            A budget that size lets the generator reach real
#                            dragons, which is what makes the red side legible.
#   s ESC                    stow it and close the manager, which refuses to
#                            close with an item in the air.
#   b a                      blast, choose the wand. There is no direction to
#                            give: an EA_SUMMON wand places its creature next
#                            to the caster and never prompts. An earlier
#                            version of this script typed a direction after
#                            'a'; the key became a movement command, the move
#                            printed its own message, and that message wrote
#                            over the summon line the check had to read.
#
# The wand is fired EIGHT times, and every shot is photographed. It has to be.
# Firing a wand is a Use Magic check against DC 10 + itemlevel/2
# (src/Magic.cpp:3838), which is DC 15 for a +5 wand, and an orc barbarian
# makes about one in two. A single shot would therefore measure nothing half
# the time. The check reads the shots in order and takes the FIRST one that
# summoned anything, so eight shots leave about one run in 250 with no
# reading at all. The wand carries 31 charges and cannot run dry first.

@include chargen.keys

w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN*7
SPACE
DOWN*3
SPACE
ESC
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*4
@until "Wand of Goblinoid Summoning" DOWN
ENTER
5
ENTER
@dump:wand-in-hand
s
ESC
b
a
@dump:fired1
b
a
@dump:fired2
b
a
@dump:fired3
b
a
@dump:fired4
b
a
@dump:fired5
b
a
@dump:fired6
b
a
@dump:fired7
b
a
@dump:fired8
